div.pre:
The current polling code unconditionally waits 30us, which was sized for a ~25us Clause 22 transaction, and then polls MDIO_CMD with a sleep of up to 2ms between samples.
Adding Clause 45 support means that every access becomes two back to back transactions (an ADDR cycle followed by a READ/WRITE cycle), so a fixed delay sized for a single C22 transaction is no longer meaningful, and a sleep of up to 2ms between samples can add significant latency to each access when the transaction has not completed at the first sample.
Instead, poll the MDIO_START_BUSY bit every microsecond so that the completion is noticed as soon as it happens, regardless of the type of transaction. This turns the wait into a busy wait, which is bounded by a timeout reduced from 100ms to 1ms: a 64 bit MDIO frame takes about 33us at the lowest MDIO clock the driver can program from a 250MHz reference clock, so even a two frame C45 access completes well within that.
Reviewed-by: Andrew Lunn <user20@example.test> Reviewed-by: Nicolai Buchwitz <user69@example.test> Co-developed-by: Carlos Soto <user66@example.test> Signed-off-by: Carlos Soto <user66@example.test> Signed-off-by: Florian Fainelli <user63@example.test> --- drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c | 23 +++++++++++++++-------- 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)
diff --git a/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c b/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c index 5f79f31220f2..df267bb82696 100644 --- a/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c +++ b/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c @@ -6,8 +6,8 @@ */
#include <linux/clk.h> -#include <linux/delay.h> #include <linux/io.h> +#include <linux/iopoll.h> #include <linux/kernel.h> #include <linux/module.h> #include <linux/of.h> @@ -36,6 +36,13 @@ #define MDIO_CLK_DIV_MASK 0x3F #define MDIO_SUPP_PREAMBLE (1 << 12)
+/* A 64 bit MDIO frame takes ~25us at 2.5MHz and ~33us at the lowest MDIO + * clock the driver can program from a 250MHz reference clock. A C45 access is + * two frames, so a 1ms timeout leaves a wide margin. + */ +#define MDIO_POLL_DELAY_US 1 +#define MDIO_POLL_TIMEOUT_US 1000 + struct unimac_mdio_priv { struct mii_bus *mii_bus; void __iomem *base; @@ -79,14 +86,14 @@ static int unimac_mdio_poll(void *wait_func_data) struct unimac_mdio_priv *priv = wait_func_data; u32 val;
- /* - * C22 transactions should take ~25 usec, will need to adjust - * if C45 support is added. + /* C22 transactions should take ~25 usec, C45 transactions take about + * twice as long since they need an address and a data cycle. */ - udelay(30); - - return read_poll_timeout(unimac_mdio_readl, val, !(val & MDIO_START_BUSY), - 2000, 100000, false, priv, MDIO_CMD); + return read_poll_timeout_atomic(unimac_mdio_readl, val, + !(val & MDIO_START_BUSY), + MDIO_POLL_DELAY_US, + MDIO_POLL_TIMEOUT_US, false, priv, + MDIO_CMD); }
static int unimac_mdio_read(struct mii_bus *bus, int phy_id, int reg) -- 2.34.1

The MDIO_CMD operation code field starts at bit 26. Define MDIO_OP_SHIFT for it and use it to build the opcodes instead of open coding the shift.
While at it, rename MDIO_RD and MDIO_WR to MDIO_RD_C22 and MDIO_WR_C22 to make it clear that these are the Clause 22 opcodes, in preparation for adding the Clause 45 ones.
No functional change.
Reviewed-by: Andrew Lunn <user20@example.test> Reviewed-by: Nicolai Buchwitz <user69@example.test> Signed-off-by: Florian Fainelli <user63@example.test> --- drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c | 10 ++++++---- 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)
diff --git a/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c b/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c index 80fb3b5b52ab..5f79f31220f2 100644 --- a/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c +++ b/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-bcm-unimac.c @@ -21,8 +21,9 @@ #define MDIO_CMD 0x00 #define MDIO_START_BUSY (1 << 29) #define MDIO_READ_FAIL (1 << 28) -#define MDIO_RD (2 << 26) -#define MDIO_WR (1 << 26) +#define MDIO_OP_SHIFT 26 +#define MDIO_RD_C22 (2 << MDIO_OP_SHIFT) +#define MDIO_WR_C22 (1 << MDIO_OP_SHIFT) #define MDIO_PMD_SHIFT 21 #define MDIO_PMD_MASK 0x1F #define MDIO_REG_SHIFT 16 @@ -99,7 +100,8 @@ static int unimac_mdio_read(struct mii_bus *bus, int phy_id, int reg) return ret;
/* Prepare the read operation */ - cmd = MDIO_RD | (phy_id << MDIO_PMD_SHIFT) | (reg << MDIO_REG_SHIFT); + cmd = MDIO_RD_C22 | (phy_id << MDIO_PMD_SHIFT) | + (reg << MDIO_REG_SHIFT); unimac_mdio_writel(priv, cmd, MDIO_CMD);
/* Start MDIO transaction */ @@ -139,7 +141,7 @@ static int unimac_mdio_write(struct mii_bus *bus, int phy_id, return ret;
/* Prepare the write operation */ - cmd = MDIO_WR | (phy_id << MDIO_PMD_SHIFT) | + cmd = MDIO_WR_C22 | (phy_id << MDIO_PMD_SHIFT) | (reg << MDIO_REG_SHIFT) | (0xffff & val); unimac_mdio_writel(priv, cmd, MDIO_CMD);
-- 2.34.1

get_phy_c22_id() maps a bus read error of -EIO or -ENODEV to -ENODEV, so
that mdiobus_scan_bus_c22() carries on with the next address when nothing
answers. get_phy_c45_ids() only does so for phy_c45_probe_present(); a
failed read of the devices in package registers is turned into -EIO, which
mdiobus_scan_bus_c45() treats as fatal and which makes the whole
__mdiobus_register() fail.

MDIO controllers that report a read from an empty address as a bus error
(e.g. MDIO_READ_FAIL on the UniMAC MDIO block) and that provide read_c45()
with unmasked addresses therefore cannot be registered: the C45 scan fails
on the first empty address.

Let get_phy_c45_devs_in_pkg() return the bus error and apply the same
mapping as get_phy_c22_id() to the two places that look for a device at an
address: the loop over the MMDs and the MMD 0 fallback. Errors from the
reads of the per-MMD identifiers, which only happen once a device has
answered, are still reported as -EIO.

Signed-off-by: Florian Fainelli <user63@example.test>
---
 drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c | 24 +++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c b/drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c
index b39e945cebd7..4071041e1051 100644
--- a/drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c
@@ -780,7 +780,7 @@ static int phy_c45_probe_present(struct mii_bus *bus, int prtad, int devad)
  * Description: reads devices in package registers of a MMD at @dev_addr
  * from PHY at @addr on @bus.
  *
- * Returns: 0 on success, -EIO on failure.
+ * Returns: 0 on success, or the negative error code from the bus read.
  */
 static int get_phy_c45_devs_in_pkg(struct mii_bus *bus, int addr, int dev_addr,
 				   u32 *devices_in_package)
@@ -789,17 +789,30 @@ static int get_phy_c45_devs_in_pkg(struct mii_bus *bus, int addr, int dev_addr,

 	phy_reg = mdiobus_c45_read(bus, addr, dev_addr, MDIO_DEVS2);
 	if (phy_reg < 0)
-		return -EIO;
+		return phy_reg;
 	*devices_in_package = phy_reg << 16;

 	phy_reg = mdiobus_c45_read(bus, addr, dev_addr, MDIO_DEVS1);
 	if (phy_reg < 0)
-		return -EIO;
+		return phy_reg;
 	*devices_in_package |= phy_reg;

 	return 0;
 }

+/* get_phy_c45_probe_err - map a bus error seen while looking for a device.
+ * @err: negative error code returned by a bus read
+ *
+ * Like get_phy_c22_id(), report -ENODEV when the bus read failed with -EIO or
+ * -ENODEV, which is how a controller signals that nothing answered at this
+ * address, so that bus scanning carries on with the next address. Anything
+ * else is reported as -EIO.
+ */
+static int get_phy_c45_probe_err(int err)
+{
+	return (err == -EIO || err == -ENODEV) ? -ENODEV : -EIO;
+}
+
 /**
  * get_phy_c45_ids - reads the specified addr for its 802.3-c45 IDs.
  * @bus: the target MII bus
@@ -845,7 +858,8 @@ static int get_phy_c45_ids(struct mii_bus *bus, int addr,
 		}
 		phy_reg = get_phy_c45_devs_in_pkg(bus, addr, i, &devs_in_pkg);
 		if (phy_reg < 0)
-			return -EIO;

On 16/11/2026 13:08, Xuanqiang Luo wrote:
> From: Xuanqiang Luo <user75@example.test>
> 
> mlx5e_ktls_set_feature_rx() has become harder to follow: the error
> handling is nested in the conditions and the main flow is conditional.
> 
> Handle the error cases in conditionals, keep the main flow
> unconditional, and destroy the accel TCP flow tables in a single
> place. Use goto to unlock on the early return.
> 
> No functional change intended.
> 

Thanks for following up on this.
The change is correct, I see no functional change.

However, we can simplify the code further.
See below suggestion where code is flat, the same size, and leaves the 
enable argument alone.

> Suggested-by: Simon Horman <user76@example.test>
> Link: https://lore.kernel.org/all/user77@example.test/
> Signed-off-by: Xuanqiang Luo <user75@example.test>
> ---
> Only compile-tested on top of net-next 7ccc5e05043b (arm64, W=1). I do
> not have a crypto-enabled ConnectX NIC to run it on.
> 
>   .../mellanox/mlx5/core/en_accel/ktls.c        | 23 +++++++++++--------
>   1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)
> 
> diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/mellanox/mlx5/core/en_accel/ktls.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/mellanox/mlx5/core/en_accel/ktls.c
> index 3cc1213c2f68..0f5dcd1b822e 100644
> --- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mellanox/mlx5/core/en_accel/ktls.c
> +++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mellanox/mlx5/core/en_accel/ktls.c
> @@ -137,19 +137,22 @@ int mlx5e_ktls_set_feature_rx(struct net_device *netdev, bool enable)
>   	mutex_lock(&priv->state_lock);
>   	if (enable) {
>   		err = mlx5e_accel_fs_tcp_create(priv->fs);
> -		if (!err && !priv->ktls_rx_was_enabled) {
> -			priv->ktls_rx_was_enabled = true;
> -			err = mlx5e_safe_reopen_channels(priv);
> -			if (err) {
> -				priv->ktls_rx_was_enabled = false;
> -				mlx5e_accel_fs_tcp_destroy(priv->fs);
> -			}
> +		if (err || priv->ktls_rx_was_enabled)
> +			goto out_unlock;
> +
> +		priv->ktls_rx_was_enabled = true;
> +		err = mlx5e_safe_reopen_channels(priv);
> +		if (err) {
> +			priv->ktls_rx_was_enabled = false;
> +			enable = false;
>   		}
> -	} else {
> -		mlx5e_accel_fs_tcp_destroy(priv->fs);
>   	}
> -	mutex_unlock(&priv->state_lock);
>   
> +	if (!enable)
> +		mlx5e_accel_fs_tcp_destroy(priv->fs);
> +
> +out_unlock:
> +	mutex_unlock(&priv->state_lock);
>   	return err;
>   }
>   

Suggestion:

	mutex_lock(&priv->state_lock);
	if (!enable) {
		mlx5e_accel_fs_tcp_destroy(priv->fs);
		goto out_unlock;
	}

	err = mlx5e_accel_fs_tcp_create(priv->fs);
	if (err || priv->ktls_rx_was_enabled)
		goto out_unlock;

	priv->ktls_rx_was_enabled = true;
	err = mlx5e_safe_reopen_channels(priv);
	if (err) {
		priv->ktls_rx_was_enabled = false;
		mlx5e_accel_fs_tcp_destroy(priv->fs);
	}

out_unlock:
	mutex_unlock(&priv->state_lock);
	return err;

Let the user change the primary dev_addr used by the device. Core
doesn't schedule an rx mode update for an address change, so the TCAM is
updated inline. The device address entry is rewritten in place, so
frames to the old address stop matching once the new one is written.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Zahka <user89@example.test>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c
index c01e276c4a74..fff35d150572 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c
@@ -83,6 +83,25 @@ static int mpnic_set_rx_mode(struct net_device *netdev,
 	return 0;
 }

+static int mpnic_set_mac(struct net_device *netdev, void *p)
+{
+	struct mpnic_net *mpn = netdev_priv(netdev);
+	struct sockaddr *addr = p;
+
+	if (!is_valid_ether_addr(addr->sa_data))
+		return -EADDRNOTAVAIL;
+
+	eth_hw_addr_set(netdev, addr->sa_data);
+
+	if (netif_running(netdev)) {
+		netif_addr_lock_bh(netdev);
+		__mpnic_set_rx_mode(mpn->mpd, &netdev->uc, &netdev->mc);
+		netif_addr_unlock_bh(netdev);
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static void mpnic_clear_rx_mode(struct mpnic_dev *mpd)
 {
 	struct mpnic_mac_addr *mac_addr = mpd->mac_addr;
@@ -237,6 +256,7 @@ static const struct net_device_ops mpnic_netdev_ops = {
 	.ndo_open		= mpnic_open,
 	.ndo_stop		= mpnic_stop,
 	.ndo_validate_addr	= eth_validate_addr,
+	.ndo_set_mac_address	= mpnic_set_mac,
 	.ndo_start_xmit		= mpnic_xmit_frame,
 	.ndo_features_check	= mpnic_features_check,
 	.ndo_set_rx_mode_async	= mpnic_set_rx_mode,

-- 
2.52.1

Add the secondary unicast and multicast addresses to the MACDA TCAM.
Advertise IFF_UNICAST_FLT so we don't need to use promiscuous mode to
handle extra unicast addresses.

When the unicast or multicast list outgrows the table, fall back to
promiscuous or all-multicast mode respectively.

Track the number of users of a HW filter, because the same address can
be used in the dev->uc and dev->mc lists.

Allow a filter slot pending deletion to be reused during
mpnic_macda_sync(). Document this transition in the state machine
comment.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Zahka <user89@example.test>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c | 38 ++++++++++++++--
 drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_rpc.c    | 60 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_rpc.h    |  5 +++
 3 files changed, 100 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c
index 43547a7d6e5c..c01e276c4a74 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c
@@ -12,12 +12,30 @@
 #include "mpnic_netdev.h"
 #include "mpnic_txrx.h"

+static int mpnic_addr_sync(struct net_device *netdev,
+			   const unsigned char *addr)
+{
+	struct mpnic_net *mpn = netdev_priv(netdev);
+
+	return mpnic_macda_sync(mpn->mpd, addr);
+}
+
+static int mpnic_addr_unsync(struct net_device *netdev,
+			     const unsigned char *addr)
+{
+	struct mpnic_net *mpn = netdev_priv(netdev);
+
+	return mpnic_macda_unsync(mpn->mpd, addr);
+}
+
 static void __mpnic_set_rx_mode(struct mpnic_dev *mpd,
+				struct netdev_hw_addr_list *uc,
 				struct netdev_hw_addr_list *mc)
 {
 	bool uc_promisc = false, mc_promisc = false;
 	struct net_device *netdev = mpd->netdev;
 	struct mpnic_mac_addr *mac_addr;
+	int err;

 	mac_addr = &mpd->mac_addr[MPNIC_TCAM_MACDA_HOST_ADDR_IDX];
 	if (!ether_addr_equal(mac_addr->value, netdev->dev_addr) ||
@@ -35,7 +53,14 @@ static void __mpnic_set_rx_mode(struct mpnic_dev *mpd,
 		mac_addr->state = MPNIC_TCAM_S_ADD;
 	}

-	if (!netdev_hw_addr_list_empty(mc))
+	err = __hw_addr_sync_dev(uc, netdev, mpnic_addr_sync,
+				 mpnic_addr_unsync);
+	if (err == -ENOSPC)
+		uc_promisc = true;
+
+	err = __hw_addr_sync_dev(mc, netdev, mpnic_addr_sync,
+				 mpnic_addr_unsync);
+	if (err == -ENOSPC)
 		mc_promisc = true;

 	uc_promisc |= netdev->flags & IFF_PROMISC;
@@ -53,7 +78,7 @@ static int mpnic_set_rx_mode(struct net_device *netdev,
 	struct mpnic_net *mpn = netdev_priv(netdev);

 	if (netif_running(netdev))
-		__mpnic_set_rx_mode(mpn->mpd, mc);
+		__mpnic_set_rx_mode(mpn->mpd, uc, mc);

 	return 0;
 }
@@ -61,6 +86,12 @@ static int mpnic_set_rx_mode(struct net_device *netdev,
 static void mpnic_clear_rx_mode(struct mpnic_dev *mpd)
 {
 	struct mpnic_mac_addr *mac_addr = mpd->mac_addr;
+	struct net_device *netdev = mpd->netdev;
+
+	netif_addr_lock_bh(netdev);
+	__dev_uc_unsync(netdev, mpnic_addr_unsync);

Implement IFF_PROMISC and IFF_ALLMULTI with the last TCAM entry, which
matches either every address or only the multicast bit.

Change the default action on TCAM misses to drop frames. With the
device, broadcast and promiscuous entries in place, frames that miss the
TCAM can be dropped instead of delivered to the stack to drop.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Zahka <user89@example.test>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_init.c   |  5 +----
 drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++---
 drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_rpc.c    | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_rpc.h    |  3 +++
 4 files changed, 49 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_init.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_init.c
index 889555eb4790..0c2878c18c54 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_init.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_init.c
@@ -199,12 +199,9 @@ static void mpnic_txb_init(struct mpnic_dev *mpd)

 static void mpnic_rxb_init(struct mpnic_dev *mpd)
 {
-	/* Accept all packets that miss dmac tcam, until l2 filtering is
-	 * implemented.
-	 */
 	mpnic_wr64(mpd, MPNIC_RXB_PORT_CLASS_CFG(0),
 		   FIELD_PREP(MPNIC_RXB_PORT_CLASS_CFG_DEFAULT_L2_ACTION,
-			      MPNIC_L2_ACTION_PASS));
+			      MPNIC_L2_ACTION_DROP));
 	mpnic_wr64(mpd, MPNIC_RXB_PORT_CFG(0),
 		   FIELD_PREP(MPNIC_RXB_PORT_CFG_FCS_STRIP_MODE,
 			      MPNIC_FCS_MODE_STRIP));
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c
index 16611813db55..43547a7d6e5c 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c
@@ -12,8 +12,10 @@
 #include "mpnic_netdev.h"
 #include "mpnic_txrx.h"

-static void __mpnic_set_rx_mode(struct mpnic_dev *mpd)
+static void __mpnic_set_rx_mode(struct mpnic_dev *mpd,
+				struct netdev_hw_addr_list *mc)
 {
+	bool uc_promisc = false, mc_promisc = false;
 	struct net_device *netdev = mpd->netdev;
 	struct mpnic_mac_addr *mac_addr;

@@ -33,6 +35,14 @@ static void __mpnic_set_rx_mode(struct mpnic_dev *mpd)
 		mac_addr->state = MPNIC_TCAM_S_ADD;
 	}

+	if (!netdev_hw_addr_list_empty(mc))
+		mc_promisc = true;
+
+	uc_promisc |= netdev->flags & IFF_PROMISC;
+	mc_promisc |= netdev->flags & IFF_ALLMULTI;
+
+	mpnic_promisc_sync(mpd, uc_promisc, mc_promisc);
+
 	mpnic_write_macda(mpd);
 }

@@ -43,7 +53,7 @@ static int mpnic_set_rx_mode(struct net_device *netdev,
 	struct mpnic_net *mpn = netdev_priv(netdev);

 	if (netif_running(netdev))
-		__mpnic_set_rx_mode(mpn->mpd);
+		__mpnic_set_rx_mode(mpn->mpd, mc);

 	return 0;
 }
@@ -54,6 +64,7 @@ static void mpnic_clear_rx_mode(struct mpnic_dev *mpd)

 	mac_addr[MPNIC_TCAM_MACDA_BROADCAST_IDX].state = MPNIC_TCAM_S_DELETE;
 	mac_addr[MPNIC_TCAM_MACDA_HOST_ADDR_IDX].state = MPNIC_TCAM_S_DELETE;

The device checks the destination MAC of every frame against a 1024 entry TCAM in order to select a pass or drop action. Up to this point, no addresses have been programmed into the TCAM, and the default action is set to pass.
Program the dev_addr and ethernet broadcast address into the TCAM. At init, all TCAM entries are set to invalid, and all match actions are set to pass. This means adding an address and making the entry valid will result in pass action being taken for that address. When we no longer want to filter for that address, the entry is simply invalidated. We never change the per-entry match actions after initialization.
Because the default action on TCAM miss is still set to pass, the device is still effectively in promiscuous mode even after this commit.
Signed-off-by: Daniel Zahka <user89@example.test> --- drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/Makefile | 1 + drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic.h | 3 ++ drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_csr.h | 10 ++++++ drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_init.c | 10 ++++++ drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_netdev.c | 49 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++ drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_rpc.c | 42 ++++++++++++++++++++++ drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic_rpc.h | 43 ++++++++++++++++++++++ 7 files changed, 158 insertions(+)
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/Makefile b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/Makefile index d5bdbd5bd1c7..1910df806d77 100644 --- a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/Makefile +++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/Makefile @@ -12,5 +12,6 @@ mpnic-y := \ mpnic_irq.o \ mpnic_netdev.o \ mpnic_pci.o \ + mpnic_rpc.o \ mpnic_txrx.o \ # End of mpnic-y diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic.h b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic.h index 8b08af0fadcc..9e31bb71aa94 100644 --- a/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic.h +++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/meta/mpnic/mpnic.h @@ -10,6 +10,7 @@ #include <linux/workqueue.h>
#include "mpnic_csr.h" +#include "mpnic_rpc.h"
#define MPNIC_DRV_NAME "mpnic"
@@ -36,6 +37,8 @@ struct mpnic_dev { u32 mps; u32 readrq; u8 relaxed_ord; + + struct mpnic_mac_addr mac_addr[MPNIC_RXB_DMAC_TCAM_CNT]; };
